course: dictionary
lesson: 13954
title: Lidded: a lid, or sleepy eyes
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Many words are built from a smaller word plus a small ending. Lidded comes from lid, the cover on top of a box or pot, plus -ed, which turns it into a describing word: having a lid.
explain: «She kept the soup in a lidded pot so it would not spill.» The word lid tells you the pot has a cover on top, so it is a safe pot for hot food.
explain: Stories use lidded in another way. «His heavy-lidded eyes closed slowly in the warm room.» Lidded eyes are half-closed, with heavy eyelids, as if sleepy. Look at the rest of the sentence for clues about how the character feels.
fact: Lidded means having a lid, a cover on top.
fact: «A lidded box» keeps things safe inside and dust out.
fact: «A lidded cup» is safer for hot drinks because the cover stops spills.
fact: «Heavy-lidded eyes» are half-closed, with heavy eyelids, as if sleepy.
fact: Lid is the root word, and adding -ed makes it describe something that has a lid.
exercise: «Mum poured hot cocoa into a lidded cup.» What does lidded mean here? | Made of glass | Having a handle on the side | *Having a cover on top | Very hot and steaming
exercise: «The baby slept with heavy-lidded eyes.» What do heavy-lidded eyes suggest? | Angry and shouting | *Sleepy or calm | Surprised by a loud noise | Wide awake and cross
exercise: Find the root word inside lidded. | ledge | bidded | *lid | lad
test: «The lidded bin kept the smell inside.» What does lidded mean? | Full of rubbish | *Having a lid on top | Open to the sky | Painted bright blue
test: Which sentence shows someone is sleepy? | The lidded pot boiled over. | The lidded box was painted red. | The lidded cup stood on the table. | *Her heavy-lidded eyes closed slowly after lunch.
exam: What do «heavy-lidded eyes» most likely show? | Anger at the teacher | *Tiredness or calm | Fear of the dark | Joy at a party
